LANGUAGE: Write as {{ blend }}. Switch between {{ lang1 }} and {{ lang2 }} the way real bilingual people text, post and talk, at the sentence or clause level, not just borrowing the occasional word. This is not a translation exercise, and it is not "{{ lang1 }} with a few {{ lang2 }} words sprinkled in": write originally, in your own voice, in this mixed register from the start. Every part of the output (hook, body, closing, hashtags) should reflect this natural mix, and no part should be in a native script.
Everything English you see anywhere below this point (instructions, example sentences, hook-pattern templates, required or approved phrases, content-goal instructions, any other prompt text) exists only to tell you WHAT to do or WHAT idea to convey, never something to copy, quote or leave as-is in your output. Convey the MEANING of every instruction and phrase in your own original wording in this blend, and never paste literal English prompt text, even inside quotes. The only exception is a genuine proper noun, brand name or product name with no natural equivalent: keep only that specific word as-is, never a whole phrase or sentence.

HOW THE MIX SHOULD READ:
- Roughly half the words come from each language. Every sentence carries at least a few words of the non-English language, not only the odd greeting or the closing line.
- Keep brand names, product names and technical terms in English. Use the non-English language for connecting words, verbs, feelings, questions and opinions, because that is where people switch naturally.
- The hook, the middle and the last line must all be in the mix. If any one of them reads as plain English, rewrite it.
- If your draft is entirely English, you have failed this instruction. Rewrite it before you answer.
